
  [1mFork 1 — how the code loads.[0m The repo has two incompatible module styles: [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is a plain [38;5;153m<script src>[39m with bare
  globals and no exports, [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m is CommonJS ([38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m/[38;5;153mrequire[39m). A browser-facing preferences module has to
  pick one, and the choice is not cheap to reverse — it decides whether [38;5;153msrc/[39m code can reuse the module or whether you
  maintain two copies.

  - [1mES modules[0m ([38;5;153mexport[39m/[38;5;153mimport[39m, [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m) — buys: one module usable by both the browser and modern Node,
    real imports instead of globals, testable in isolation. Costs: [38;5;153mindex.html[39m needs [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m, and [38;5;153mapp.js[39m stops
    being a bare global script. Rules out nothing.
  - [1mPlain global script[0m (matches today's [38;5;153mapp.js[39m) — buys: zero disruption, no HTML change. Costs: can't be imported by
    [38;5;153msrc/[39m, can't be unit-tested without a DOM shim, pollutes [38;5;153mwindow[39m. Rules out reuse and clean tests.
  - [1mCommonJS[0m (matches [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m) — buys: consistency with [38;5;153msrc/[39m. Costs: doesn't run in a browser without a bundler,
    and adding a bundler is a bigger dependency than this whole feature.

  [1mMy recommendation: ES modules.[0m It's the only option where one preferences module serves both halves of the repo and
  can be tested without a browser. The cost is one attribute in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m.

  [1mFork 2 — the preference set.[0m You picked typed-with-defaults, so I need the actual list. One hard constraint regardless
  of what you pick: [1mthe password never goes into storage.[0m A "remember username" preference stores only the username
  string. Persisting a password to localStorage would put a credential in plaintext where any script on the page can
  read it, and I won't design that in.

